language js

// `absentValue<T>()` / `nullValue<T>()` — `undefined` and `null` handed to a
// parameter whose type forbids them, via the single `coerce` type-lie in
// test/helpers/absent.ts. One helper, so one place to audit, but every call
// site is still an assertion that a type says is impossible.
//
// Scoped to `test/**` in biome.json. test/helpers/absent.ts declares the two
// functions rather than calling them, so it does not match this pattern and
// needs no exemption — unlike the regex counter, which exempts it by path.
//
// This is the ONLY gate on the idiom. It replaced the `absentValue` counter in
// scripts/check-test-escape-hatches.ts, which retired on 2026-08-27: that
// counter was text, so it matched the identifiers inside the scanner's own
// string fixtures (and had to exempt them by path), missed any call reached
// through a namespace or an alias, and its whole remaining residue was prose.
// A regex kept as a "secondary guard" behind a working rule guards nothing.
//
// Because nothing else watches this shape, the wiring is pinned behind
// test/unit/scripts/biome-no-absent-value-plugin.test.ts. Keep it that way:
// delete the `plugins` entry or move the `test/**` override and the gate stops
// firing silently.

`$fn<$_>()` where {
  or {
    $fn <: `absentValue`,
    $fn <: `nullValue`
  },
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $fn,
    message = "`absentValue<T>()` / `nullValue<T>()` assert a state the type forbids. Ask whether the parameter should admit `undefined` (the src type is wrong) or whether the absence is impossible after validation (the test is wrong) — see .nax/rules/test-ratchets.md."
  )
}
